hp 15.339 | Think of a sheet of paper. Fold it around a wide curve, and it opens u
he 14.549 | At the ends, the hinge is flat, under a titanium cover strong enough t
  hs: As a whole, the hinge splits into six jobs, hinges that let each side rotate, springs and cams that hold it, gears that keep both sides in step, and a channel in the middle for the cables. Most of it is mirrored top and bottom.
  h4: Next to the gears are springs pressing on cams. When the phone is open, the cams lock together and hold it flat. As you fold it, the cams squeeze the springs, and the friction holds the phone at any angle from about 15 to 165 degrees, half open like a tiny laptop or standing like a tent.
  hm: and in the last few degrees, magnets in the frame take over and pull the two halves shut.
  h5: When the phone closes, the screen doesn't fold flat. It curves into a teardrop-shaped loop, tucked inside the hinge, almost four millimeters across.
  hp: Think of a sheet of paper. Fold it around a wide curve and it opens up smooth. Press it flat and you get a sharp crease. That's why, on a new duo, the crease is only about 48 microns deep, half the thickness of a sheet of paper.
  he: At the ends the hinge is flat, under a titanium cover strong enough to take a knock, with no bumps to catch your finger, and even with all these moving parts, the phone is rated IP sixty eight against dust and water.
