d2 16.939 | Why does thin bend more easily? When a sheet bends, its outside stretc
d3 20.693 | On top is a flexible polymer cover. Under a microscope, its surface is
dg 13.888 | Under it is a layer of ultra-thin glass, thinner than a sheet of paper
d10 14.272 | At the bottom, a plastic base bends instead of shattering, a soft foam
  d1: A normal phone screen sits under thick, hard glass. Glass like that can't fold, so the inner screen is a flexible OLED panel built from more than ten ultra thin layers and adhesives.
  d2: Why does thin bend more easily? When a sheet bends, its outside stretches and its inside squeezes. The thinner the sheet, the less each surface has to stretch. That's why every layer here is made as thin as possible. Even glass can bend if it's thin enough.
  d3: On top is a flexible polymer cover. Under a microscope, its surface is covered in tiny bumps, the nanotexture. It scatters reflections, so the screen shows less glare and the reflection off the crease almost disappears. It also makes the surface harder to scratch, and this cover can be replaced on its own.
  dg: Under it is a layer of ultra thin glass, thinner than a sheet of paper, and there's another one below the panel. Glass this thin bends and it spreads the pressure of a fingertip or a pencil, so the screen resists dents.
  d10: At the bottom, a plastic base bends instead of shattering, a soft foam cushion absorbs the press of your finger, and a metal support plate gives the whole panel its strength. Next, how one phone runs on two batteries.
